==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|overheren}}, from {{inh|en|ang|oferhīeran|t=to overhear, hear, disobey, disregard, neglect}}, equivalent to {{pre|en|over-|hear}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|overhoren|t=to hear, hear about}}, {{cog|de|überhören|t=to not hear, ignore}}, {{cog|da|overhøre|t=to overhear}}, {{cog|is|yfirheyra|t=to hear}}, {{cog|got|*𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽|t=to disregard, disobey}} (in {{m|got|𐌿𐍆𐌰𐍂𐌷𐌰𐌿𐍃𐌴𐌹𐌽𐍃}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/oʊ.vɚˈhɪɚ/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-overhear.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪə(ɹ)|s=3}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|overhears|overhearing|overheard}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[hear]] something that was not meant for one's ears.
#: {{ux|en|I was hanging clothes in the garden and I '''overheard''' the neighbours talking about Sheila's pregnancy.}}

====Hyponyms====
* {{l|en|eavesdrop}}<!--the type of overhearing that is intentionally achieved-->

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|overhearer}}

